<h1>4.2 IDistributedCache and Redis</h1>
<p class="lead"><code>IDistributedCache</code> is the .NET abstraction for a shared cache. Your code talks to the interface, and configuration decides whether the bytes go to Redis, SQL Server or local memory.</p>

<div class="story" data-story="4.2"></div>


<details class="deep">
<summary>&#128214; Want to understand it deeply? Read the full explanation</summary>

<h2>The interface</h2>
<p>It is deliberately small. It stores <b>byte arrays</b> (plus string helpers), with absolute and sliding expiration:</p>
<pre><code>Task&lt;byte[]?&gt; GetAsync(string key);
Task SetAsync(string key, byte[] value, DistributedCacheEntryOptions options);
Task RefreshAsync(string key);
Task RemoveAsync(string key);</code></pre>
<p><code>RefreshAsync</code> resets the sliding expiration without reading the value. There is no "get or create", no tags and no stampede protection: those come with HybridCache in lesson 4.4.</p>

<h2>Implementations</h2>
<div class="card">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Registration</th><th>Storage</th><th>When to use</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>AddStackExchangeRedisCache</code></td><td>Redis</td><td>The usual production choice. Fast, rich features.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>AddDistributedSqlServerCache</code></td><td>A SQL Server table</td><td>You already have SQL Server and want no new infrastructure. Slower than Redis.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>AddDistributedMemoryCache</code></td><td>Local memory</td><td>Development and tests. Not really distributed. This project falls back to it when no Redis is configured.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Others (NCache, Cosmos DB, PostgreSQL...)</td><td>Varies</td><td>Community and vendor packages implementing the same interface.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p>In this project <code>Caching.Infrastructure/DependencyInjection.cs</code> decides:</p>
<pre><code>if (redis.IsConfigured)
    services.AddStackExchangeRedisCache(options =&gt;
    {
        options.InstanceName = "caching-academy:";
        options.ConnectionMultiplexerFactory = () =&gt; Task.FromResult(redis.Multiplexer);
    });
else
    services.AddDistributedMemoryCache();</code></pre>
<p><code>InstanceName</code> is a prefix added to every key, which keeps this app's keys apart from anything else in the same Redis database. The factory reuses the app's single Redis connection: <code>ConnectionMultiplexer</code> is designed to be created once and shared.</p>

<h2>How Redis stores an IDistributedCache entry</h2>
<p>Each entry is a Redis <b>hash</b> with three fields: <code>data</code> (your bytes), <code>absexp</code> (absolute expiry in ticks) and <code>sldexp</code> (sliding window in ticks). Redis's own TTL on the key is set to the sliding window, and refreshed on each read. Use the inspector below to see a real entry.</p>

<h2>A cache must fail open</h2>
<p>Redis is a separate server, reached over the network. It can be slow, restarting or unreachable. A cache is an optimization, so when it fails the app should <b>fall back to the database</b>, not return errors. <code>DistributedProductService</code> wraps every cache call:</p>
<pre><code>try
{
    var bytes = await cache.GetAsync(key);
    ...
}
catch (Exception ex) when (ex is not OperationCanceledException)
{
    var product = await repository.GetByIdAsync(id);
    return product.ToResult("Database", key, stopwatch, "Cache unavailable, the app kept working");
}</code></pre>
<p>Timeouts matter too: the Redis connection uses 2-second timeouts, so a dead Redis costs each request at most about 2 seconds, not the default 5. Level 7 adds circuit-breaker ideas.</p>

</details>

