<h2>Experiments: do these in order</h2>
<ol class="steps">
    <li><b>Miss and hit.</b> Click <em>Get</em> twice. The hit takes about 1 to 5 ms (or more on the free cloud Redis, depending on distance), much slower than IMemoryCache's microseconds, but much faster than the 800 ms database.</li>
    <li><b>Look inside Redis.</b> Click <em>Inspect in Redis</em>. You see the key with the <code>caching-academy:</code> prefix removed, its type <code>hash</code>, the TTL and the three fields. <code>data</code> is the JSON of the product.</li>
    <li><b>Sliding expiration.</b> Inspect again a few seconds later: the TTL goes down. Click <em>Refresh</em> and inspect: the TTL is back to about 30 seconds. Reading with <em>Get</em> also refreshes it. The entry still dies after 120 seconds at most (absolute).</li>
    <li><b>Restart survival.</b> If you run the app locally, restart it and click <em>Get</em>: still a hit. The cache lives in Redis, not in the app.</li>
    <li><b>Failure.</b> If Redis is unreachable, <em>Get</em> still returns the product from the database with a note. Nothing breaks.</li>
</ol>

<h2>What you should take away</h2>
<div class="callout tip">
    <strong>Key points</strong>
    Code against <code>IDistributedCache</code> and choose the store in configuration. It stores bytes, so you serialize yourself. Reuse one Redis connection, prefix your keys, set short timeouts and always fall back to the source when the cache fails.
</div>
<div class="callout danger">
    <strong>Common mistakes</strong>
    Creating a new <code>ConnectionMultiplexer</code> per request (it is expensive and exhausts connections). Letting a Redis outage turn into a site outage. Forgetting that a hit now costs a network round trip, so caching tiny, cheap values in Redis can be slower than just computing them.
</div>

